WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.500
- [Instructor] We're told this
is the graph of function f,

00:02.500 --> 00:03.380
fair enough.

00:03.380 --> 00:07.270
Function g is defined as g
of x is equal to f of two x.

00:07.270 --> 00:08.690
What is the graph of g?

00:08.690 --> 00:12.120
So pause this video, and try
to figure that out on your own.

00:12.120 --> 00:13.490
All right, now let's work through this.

00:13.490 --> 00:15.390
And the way I will think about it,

00:15.390 --> 00:17.240
I'll set up a little table here.

00:17.240 --> 00:18.933
And I'll have an x column,

00:21.089 --> 00:24.680
and then I'll have a, well,
actually just put g of x column.

00:24.680 --> 00:28.370
And of course, g of x
is equal to f of two x.

00:28.370 --> 00:31.210
So when x is,

00:31.210 --> 00:33.520
and actually let me
see, when x is equal to,

00:33.520 --> 00:36.540
I could pick a point like x equaling zero,

00:36.540 --> 00:41.420
so g of zero is going to
be f of two times zero.

00:41.420 --> 00:43.560
So it's going to be f of two times zero,

00:43.560 --> 00:45.720
which is still f of zero,

00:45.720 --> 00:49.490
which is going to be equal
to a little bit over four,

00:49.490 --> 00:53.180
so which is equal to f of zero.

00:53.180 --> 00:57.930
And so they're going to both
have the same y-intercept,

00:57.930 --> 01:00.110
but interesting things are
going to happen the further

01:00.110 --> 01:02.090
that we get from the y-axis

01:02.090 --> 01:05.160
or as our x increases in
either direction away,

01:05.160 --> 01:08.720
or as our x gets bigger in
either direction from zero.

01:08.720 --> 01:11.160
So let's think about
what's going to happen

01:11.160 --> 01:13.770
at x equals two.

01:13.770 --> 01:15.120
So at x equals two,

01:15.120 --> 01:19.490
g of two is going to be
equal to f of two times two,

01:19.490 --> 01:22.120
two times two, which
is equal to f of four.

01:22.120 --> 01:24.160
And we know what f of four is.

01:24.160 --> 01:26.750
F of four is equal to zero.

01:26.750 --> 01:31.050
So g of two is equal to f of
four, which is equal to zero.

01:31.050 --> 01:33.400
So notice, the corresponding
point has kind of

01:33.400 --> 01:36.610
gotten compressed in or
squeezed in or squished in,

01:36.610 --> 01:38.550
in the horizontal direction.

01:38.550 --> 01:39.860
And so what you see happening,

01:39.860 --> 01:41.660
at least on this side of the graph,

01:41.660 --> 01:43.960
is everything's happening
a little bit faster.

01:45.230 --> 01:46.610
Whatever was happening at a certain x,

01:46.610 --> 01:48.770
it's now happening at half of that x.

01:48.770 --> 01:51.590
So this side of the graph
is going to look something,

01:51.590 --> 01:54.060
try to draw it a little
bit better than that,

01:54.060 --> 01:56.300
it's going to look something

01:57.926 --> 01:59.503
like this,

01:59.503 --> 02:00.336
like this.

02:01.300 --> 02:03.850
Everything's happening twice as fast.

02:03.850 --> 02:06.180
And what happens when you go
in the negative direction?

02:06.180 --> 02:08.800
Well, think about what
g of negative two is.

02:08.800 --> 02:13.570
G of negative two is equal to
f of two times negative two,

02:13.570 --> 02:14.850
two times negative two,

02:14.850 --> 02:17.240
which is equal to f of negative four,

02:17.240 --> 02:18.960
which we see is also equal to zero.

02:18.960 --> 02:20.990
So g of negative two is zero.

02:20.990 --> 02:22.217
And you might be thinking,

02:22.217 --> 02:24.260
"Why did you pick two and negative two?"

02:24.260 --> 02:25.580
Well, the intuition is

02:25.580 --> 02:27.100
that things are going to be squeezed in.

02:27.100 --> 02:29.080
Things are happening twice as fast.

02:29.080 --> 02:30.730
So whatever was happening at x equals four

02:30.730 --> 02:32.020
is now going to happen at x equals two.

02:32.020 --> 02:33.390
Whatever is happening at
x equals negative four

02:33.390 --> 02:35.290
is now going to happen
at x equals negative two.

02:35.290 --> 02:37.620
And I saw that we were
at very clear points

02:37.620 --> 02:40.180
at x equals negative four
and x equals four on f,

02:40.180 --> 02:41.300
so I just took half of that

02:41.300 --> 02:43.970
to pick my x-values right over here.

02:43.970 --> 02:45.900
And then so what our graph is going

02:45.900 --> 02:48.203
to look like is something like this.

02:48.203 --> 02:49.920
It's going to look something like this.

02:49.920 --> 02:52.880
It's going to look like
it's been squished in

02:52.880 --> 02:55.980
from the right and the left.

02:55.980 --> 02:57.483
Now let's do another example.

02:59.100 --> 03:01.720
So now they've not only
given the graph of f,

03:01.720 --> 03:04.960
they've given an expression for it.

03:04.960 --> 03:09.190
What is the graph of g of x

03:09.190 --> 03:10.680
which is equal to this business?

03:10.680 --> 03:13.780
So pause this video, and
try to figure that out.

03:13.780 --> 03:15.440
All right, the key is to
figure out the relationship

03:15.440 --> 03:18.040
between f of x and g of x.

03:18.040 --> 03:21.830
And what we can see,
the main difference is,

03:21.830 --> 03:25.640
is instead of an x here in f
of x, we have an x over two.

03:25.640 --> 03:26.900
So everywhere there was an x,

03:26.900 --> 03:28.820
we've been replaced with an x over two.

03:28.820 --> 03:32.450
So another way of thinking
about it is g of x

03:32.450 --> 03:37.450
is equal to f of not
x but f of x over two.

03:38.260 --> 03:40.640
Or another way of
thinking about it, g of x

03:40.640 --> 03:42.423
is equal to f of 1/2x.

03:44.000 --> 03:47.280
And then we can do a
similar type of exercise.

03:47.280 --> 03:49.620
And they've given us
some interesting points,

03:49.620 --> 03:52.650
the points two, the point,

03:52.650 --> 03:55.230
or the point x equals two,
the point x equals four,

03:55.230 --> 03:57.310
and the point x equals six.

03:57.310 --> 03:58.930
So let's think about this.

03:58.930 --> 04:01.810
Last time, when it was g
of x is equal to two x,

04:01.810 --> 04:03.890
things were happening twice as fast.

04:03.890 --> 04:06.450
Now things are going
to happen half as fast.

04:06.450 --> 04:08.460
And so what I would do,

04:08.460 --> 04:11.090
let me just set up a little table here.

04:11.090 --> 04:13.760
The interesting x-values for me

04:13.760 --> 04:17.350
are the ones that if I take half of them,

04:17.350 --> 04:19.440
then I'm going to get one of these points.

04:19.440 --> 04:21.943
So actually let me write this, half, 1/2x,

04:23.330 --> 04:25.455
and then I can think about what g of x

04:25.455 --> 04:29.000
is equal to f of 1/2x is going to be.

04:29.000 --> 04:31.820
So I want my 1/2x to be,

04:31.820 --> 04:34.290
let's see, it could be two, four, and six,

04:34.290 --> 04:36.200
two, four, and six.

04:36.200 --> 04:37.970
And why did I pick those again?

04:37.970 --> 04:41.410
Well, it's very clear
what values f takes on

04:41.410 --> 04:43.050
at those points.

04:43.050 --> 04:48.050
And so if 1/2x is two,
then x is equal to four.

04:48.050 --> 04:50.860
If 1/2x is four, then x is equal to eight.

04:50.860 --> 04:54.020
If x is equal to 12, then 1/2x is six.

04:54.020 --> 04:56.730
And so then we could
say, all right, g of four

04:56.730 --> 04:59.760
is equal to f of two,

04:59.760 --> 05:01.810
which is equal to zero.

05:01.810 --> 05:03.580
That's why I picked two, four, and six.

05:03.580 --> 05:05.790
It's very easy to evaluate f of two,

05:05.790 --> 05:07.010
f of four, and f of six.

05:07.010 --> 05:09.190
They gave us those points very clearly.

05:09.190 --> 05:13.340
So g of eight is going to be equal to g,

05:13.340 --> 05:16.630
is going to be equal to f of
1/2 of eight, or f of four,

05:16.630 --> 05:19.130
which is equal to negative four.

05:19.130 --> 05:21.390
And then g of 12

05:21.390 --> 05:23.500
is equal to f of six,

05:23.500 --> 05:26.710
which is half of 12, which
is equal to zero again.

05:26.710 --> 05:28.110
So then we could plot these points,

05:28.110 --> 05:30.930
and we get a general sense
of the shape of the graph.

05:30.930 --> 05:32.300
So let's see,

05:32.300 --> 05:36.290
g of four is equal to zero,

05:36.290 --> 05:39.770
g of eight is equal to negative four

05:39.770 --> 05:41.750
right over there,

05:41.750 --> 05:44.510
and then g of 12 is equal to zero again.

05:44.510 --> 05:47.640
So everything has been stretched out.

05:47.640 --> 05:50.810
So there you go, it's been
stretched out in at least,

05:50.810 --> 05:54.100
in the horizontal direction
is one way to think about it,

05:54.100 --> 05:55.850
in the horizontal direction.

05:55.850 --> 05:58.890
And you can see that this point

05:58.890 --> 06:01.310
in f corresponds to this point in g.

06:01.310 --> 06:04.130
It's gotten twice as far from the origin

06:04.130 --> 06:06.640
because everything is
growing half as fast.

06:06.640 --> 06:08.360
You input an x, you take a half of it,

06:08.360 --> 06:10.160
and then you input it into f.

06:10.160 --> 06:13.000
And then this point right over here

06:13.000 --> 06:14.090
corresponds to this point.

06:14.090 --> 06:15.860
Instead of happening at
four, this vertex point,

06:15.860 --> 06:17.380
it's now happening at eight.

06:17.380 --> 06:19.630
And last but not least,

06:19.630 --> 06:22.500
this point right over here
corresponds to this point.

06:22.500 --> 06:25.440
Instead of happening at
six, it's happening at 12.

06:25.440 --> 06:27.630
Everything is getting stretched out.

06:27.630 --> 06:29.363
Let's do one more example.

06:30.730 --> 06:32.170
F of x is equal to all of this.

06:32.170 --> 06:34.150
We have to be careful,
there's a cube root over here.

06:34.150 --> 06:37.490
And g is a horizontally
scaled version of f.

06:37.490 --> 06:42.260
The functions are graphed where
f is solid and g is dashed.

06:42.260 --> 06:44.380
What is the equation of g?

06:44.380 --> 06:47.590
So pause this video, and see
if you can figure that out.

06:47.590 --> 06:48.920
All right, let's do this together,

06:48.920 --> 06:50.920
and it looks like they've
given us some points

06:50.920 --> 06:52.900
that seem to correspond with each other.

06:52.900 --> 06:56.020
To go from f to g, it looks
like these corresponding points

06:56.020 --> 06:59.810
have been squeezed in
closer to the origin.

06:59.810 --> 07:03.963
And what we can see is, is
that f of negative three,

07:04.930 --> 07:06.380
f of negative three

07:06.380 --> 07:10.253
seems to be equal to g of negative one.

07:11.370 --> 07:15.280
And f of six over here,

07:15.280 --> 07:19.540
f of six seems to be equal to g of two,

07:19.540 --> 07:21.410
g of two.

07:21.410 --> 07:22.850
Or another way to think about it,

07:22.850 --> 07:26.360
whatever x you input in g,
it looks like that's going

07:26.360 --> 07:29.980
to be equivalent to three times that x

07:29.980 --> 07:32.090
inputted into f.

07:32.090 --> 07:35.280
So g of x is equal to f of three x.

07:35.280 --> 07:37.770
And so if you want to
know the equation of g,

07:37.770 --> 07:40.300
we just evaluate f of three x.

07:40.300 --> 07:43.860
So f of three x is going to be equal to,

07:43.860 --> 07:47.360
and I could just actually
put an equal sign like this,

07:47.360 --> 07:50.410
f of three x is going to
be equal to negative three

07:50.410 --> 07:52.520
times the cube root of,

07:52.520 --> 07:54.930
instead of an x, I'll put
a three x right over there,

07:54.930 --> 07:58.780
three x plus two, and
then we have plus one.

07:58.780 --> 08:01.750
And that's it, that's
what g of x is equal to.

08:01.750 --> 08:03.530
It's equal to f of three x, which is that.

08:03.530 --> 08:06.190
We substituted this x with a three x.

08:06.190 --> 08:07.883
And we are done.
